# Merge behaviour for downstream forks — Step 1 of the theme/engine split (#174).
#
# Khao Pad is designed to be forked and customised per deployment, so every
# upstream release gets merged into sites that have diverged. Analysis of a real
# fork's v3.10.0 → v4.1.0 merge found 27 conflicted files, of which roughly a
# sixth were pure noise: files that conflict every single release, carry no
# decision, and are resolved the same way every time.
#
# This file removes that class. It is the cheapest possible step — no
# architecture, no moved files, fully reversible — and it is worth doing before
# anything structural so the remaining conflicts are the ones that actually
# represent a design question.

# ── Generated lockfile ────────────────────────────────────────
# pnpm-lock.yaml conflicted on every release (573 lines in the last one) and is
# 100% regenerable. A textual merge of a lockfile is never the right answer —
# the correct resolution is always "take one side, then re-resolve":
#
#   git checkout --ours pnpm-lock.yaml && pnpm install
#
# `binary` stops git attempting a line-level merge and producing a lockfile that
# is syntactically valid, semantically wrong, and installs a dependency graph
# neither side ever tested.
#
# Deliberately NOT merge=ours. Verified in a scratch repo: with the driver
# registered, merge=ours silently keeps the fork's lockfile and reports no
# conflict at all — so an upstream dependency bump (including a security patch)
# is dropped without anyone seeing it. That is the exact silent-drift failure
# this whole effort exists to prevent.
#
# `binary` alone gives the right behaviour: git reports a conflict it cannot
# auto-resolve, and the human runs the only correct resolution:
#
#   git checkout --theirs pnpm-lock.yaml && pnpm install
#
# One deliberate action per release, and the dependency graph is actually
# re-resolved against both sides' package.json.
pnpm-lock.yaml binary linguist-generated=true

# ── Compiled output ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Paraglide compiles messages/*.json into src/lib/paraglide/. It is gitignored
# upstream, but forks have historically committed it by accident; marking it
# generated keeps it out of diffs and review noise if that happens again.
src/lib/paraglide/** linguist-generated=true

# ── Line endings ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalise to LF in the repository regardless of contributor platform. A fork
# edited on Windows can otherwise present an entire file as changed, turning a
# one-line divergence into a whole-file conflict.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary assets — never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary

# ── Deployment-owned files ────────────────────────────────────
# These exist upstream as a working default, and every deployment is expected to
# replace them wholesale. `merge=ours` keeps the deployment's version when
# merging an upstream release, so a fork stops re-resolving the same file every
# time.
#
# IMPORTANT — this only takes effect if the merge driver is registered locally:
#
#   git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# Git ships the *name* `ours` but not the driver, so without that command the
# attribute is silently ignored and you get a normal conflict. That is a safe
# failure (you notice and resolve by hand) but it is the reason this is
# documented in the README rather than assumed to work.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed here:
#
#   - (www)/+layout.svelte and the homepage — the two files #173 identifies as
#     the real pain. They need a proper override seam (Step 2), not a merge
#     strategy that silently drops upstream improvements a deployment wants.
#
#   - wrangler.toml — it carries BOTH deployment-specific bindings (D1 ids,
#     bucket names, site URLs) AND upstream additions. The v4.1.0 sync added a
#     CAREERS_FEED_URL block to it; merge=ours would have silently discarded
#     that and the feature would have looked broken for no visible reason. It
#     genuinely needs a human, every time.
#
# Only README.md qualifies: upstream's is documentation for the platform,
# a deployment's is documentation for that site, and no upstream sentence about
# Khao Pad is ever something a fork's README should adopt. Fork content belongs
# in README.md; anything a deployment wants to keep from upstream is already on
# the upstream repo.
README.md merge=ours
